An Auditable Theory Compiler for AI-Assisted
Formal Mathematics
Technical Supplement: Finite-Record-Only Safety under
Record-Gate Soundness
Haobo Ma1 and Wenlin Zhang2
1

2

AELF PTE LTD. auric@aelf.io
National University of Singapore e1327962@u.nus.edu

Abstract. Formal mathematics projects increasingly use AI agents for
drafting, review, theorem search, and manuscript preparation. Ungated
agent output, however, can create theorem-looking but content-poor edits, stale source references, duplicated submission routes, and premature paper promotion. We report an auditable theory-to-paper pipeline
used around a Lean-backed BEDC source workspace and an automath
publication workspace. The pipeline separates source development, intake packets, active paper directories, AI review loops, deterministic
gates, and human promotion decisions. Its central rule is that AI output
is advisory: load-bearing mathematical and submission claims must be
tied, at the appropriate evidence level, to source paths, command logs,
deterministic checks, and human-approved promotion. Under recordgate soundness, the core interface theorem constructs the finite publication certificates checked by the package verifier from recorded gatepassage entries and explicitly submitted review surfaces; its safety claim
is deliberately certificate-level and finite-record only. It proves neither
Lean builds, daemon behaviour, artifact semantics, nor venue acceptance.
The current package then proves finite-record certificate exactness for
its six-row, path-verified instantiation, while rejecting stronger readings
such as external gate implementation soundness, proof-assistant correctness, artifact semantic validity, current execution of every gate, or venue
acceptance. Four case studies illustrate the gates at the path-verified
historical-snapshot level: intake isolation, overlap/submitted blocking,
fake-extension rejection, and artifact-limitation disclosure.

1

Problem and contribution

AI assistance is useful in a formal-mathematics project only if the project can
distinguish proposal from evidence. A generated theorem statement, a legal piece
of code, a mechanical expansion, or a lower score in a review rubric does not by
itself constitute mathematical discovery. The practical publication failure modes
are equally structural: a route may overlap a submitted sibling, an artifact status
file may drift from generated counts, or an intake note may accidentally become
a daemon-visible manuscript.

