The setting is adjacent to proof-assistant systems such as Lean [4], machinelearning interfaces to formal proving such as LeanDojo [5], informal-to-formal
guidance methods such as Draft–Sketch–Prove [2], and large-scale formal-proof
archives such as the Archive of Formal Proofs [1]. Our focus is different: we study
the publication-facing evidence discipline around such sources.

2

Architecture

The workflow separates source work from publication work, and the separation
is the organizing contract for the rest of the supplement. BEDC source files are
treated as finite-kernel theory together with proof obligations and separate implementation contracts. Lean and related markers are formal-verification records
for named obligations, not object-language closure by themselves. Finite manifests, evaluator rows, bundled snapshots, and command logs are the substrate
witnesses from which publication certificates are built. AI agents remain advisory: they may propose, review, and triage, but they do not promote claims
into accepted theory. The automath layer is therefore a publication compiler: it
packages the source-theory interface, finite witnesses, route state, and submission
boundary without merging those evidence axes.

Definition 1 (Architecture-axis map). The compiler architecture uses five
disjoint evidence axes. An accepted claim may read evidence from an axis only
at the admissible evidence level shown in the displayed axis map; the negative
boundary in the last column is part of the claim certificate and cannot be discharged by prose from another axis.

