Proposition 2 (Axis non-escalation). In any record-gate-sound package using Definition 1, passage or evidence on one architecture axis cannot certify
a load-bearing claim whose claim kind requires another axis. More precisely, if
a claim certificate uses an axis A but its stronger reading needs an evidence
item or negative-boundary discharge listed only on a different axis A' , then the
stronger reading is not accepted unless the package contains a separate submitted
coordinate for A' and the gates assigned to that coordinate pass. Consequently
Theorem 9 constructs only finite publication certificates whose axis coordinates
are explicitly present in the submitted support surface; it does not infer BEDC
closure from Lean markers, artifact semantics from manifest rows, proof evidence from agents, venue upload from automath route state, or support-surface
availability from local bundle paths.
Proof. The operational interface records a claim by finite coordinates: claim
kind, evidence surface, evidence level, pass records assigned by G , and the boundary field. Definition 1 makes the axis of each such coordinate explicit and declares
the corresponding negative boundary. If a stronger reading needs an item on a
different axis A' , then either the claim-kind coordinate does not name the kind

6

H. Ma and W. Zhang

requiring A' , the evidence surface does not contain an admissible A' item, a required A' pass record is missing, or the boundary field still excludes the stronger
reading. In all four cases the finite record verifier of Proposition 6 rejects the
stronger reading: the dispatch table cannot assign an unmentioned gate, the surface check cannot read an unsubmitted path or log, the pass-record check cannot
be satisfied without the row, and the boundary check preserves the declared negative boundary.
Conversely, when a separate coordinate for A' is submitted, it is just another finite component of the package. It can be accepted only by the same
schema: its claim kind must dispatch to the appropriate gates, its evidence surface must list the admissible A' evidence, and its boundary must state exactly
what is and is not certified. Therefore evidence never moves between axes by
paraphrase or by agent-generated text; it moves only by adding an explicit finite
coordinate and passing the assigned gates. Applying this to the five rows of Definition 1 gives the listed consequences. They are also the axis-level instance of
the coordinate-determination result later isolated in Theorem 3: facts outside the
finite coordinates of the accepted package are not conclusions of the certificate.
The reader path is therefore architectural before it is logistical. The rest of the
supplement first fixes the source-interface spine, then defines the finite record
verifier, then proves the certificate construction and non-escalation boundary,
then instantiates that boundary on the current six-row package, and only then
lists the local case snapshots and command-run inventory. File tables are included as finite witnesses for this interface, not as a substitute for the compiler
architecture.
Concretely, the newmath source workspace contains Lean files, scripts, and
artifact sources. The automath publication workspace contains intake packets,
active paper tracks, review state, and submission metadata. A candidate first
enters an intake seed. It becomes an active paper only after a human promotion decision creates a 2026 * directory with main.tex and PIPELINE.md. This
prevents source organization from being mistaken for a submission route.
The source side supplies a discovery-gate calculus. In this calculus, statementonly and code-only candidates are insufficient; mechanical expansion and compression are not discovery by themselves; discovery claims require a semantic certificate and a ledger witness; demotion preserves source-interface audit
soundness; accepted claims must form an auditable lineage DAG; and a pipeline
is safe only when discovery claims pass the gate before being recorded as accepted work. The reviewer-facing source snapshot is review_bundle/source
