Proposition 5 (Source-interface evidence-level separation). Let r be any
accepted manuscript claim whose discovery component cites one of the imported
labels in Table 1. If r uses only Lemma 1, then the imported-label premise of r
is accepted only at the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase level. If r additionally cites Proposition 4, then the same premise is upgraded only by the finite record comparisons listed in that proposition: table–JSON equality, manifest
agreement, snapshot digest and line count agreement, and label containment in
the declared ranges. In neither case does the premise become a fresh Lean/BEDC
rebuild, a proof of the semantic truth of the imported source theorem, or an unbounded restatement of the source text. Consequently the finite-record interface
theorem, Theorem 9, can use the imported discovery-gate spine only through
these two explicitly separated evidence levels.
Proof. The operational evidence level of a cited source-interface premise is determined by the record fields actually available for that premise. Lemma 1 supplies
a pinned path, commit, extraction date, label, line range, and bounded summary for each of the ten imported labels, and its proof states that no later use
may exceed that bounded summary. Therefore a claim using only the lemma has
exactly the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase evidence listed there.
