3

Operational interface

Reader map. The supplement now separates the certificate argument into six layers. The abstract finite-record interface defines the verifier and proves the core
certificate theorem. The current-package instantiation specializes that interface
to the six displayed ledger rows. The case-study witness protocol records pathcomplete historical examples without reruns. The evidence-boundary section
then isolates the primary–supplement interface, the review-bundle or archiveequivalence condition, and the command-run boundary.
3.1

Abstract finite-record interface

For this long technical supplement and its two-page CICM presentation artifact,
the following finite interface is sufficient.
Definition 2 (Operational acceptance interface). A claim is a manuscript
assertion that can affect a mathematical, artifact, route, or submission decision.
A load-bearing claim is a claim whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the submission-readiness status. An advisory
output is any AI-agent draft, score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair
proposal before a gate records it. A deterministic gate is a repeatable predicate,
script, or checklist whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently
of the prose generated by an agent. A human promotion is the explicit editorial
act that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active 2026 * paper directory with main.tex and PIPELINE.md. An evidence surface is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs, inventories, and witness
records cited for a claim. An acceptance entry is a manuscript or submissionledger record that marks a candidate claim as usable in the publication-facing
argument. Accepted work is the subset of candidate claims with such an entry.
This is a status notion only: the definition records that an editorial acceptance

An Auditable Theory Compiler

13

entry exists, but it does not by itself assert external editorial acceptance or implementation correctness. A pipeline is record-gate-sound for acceptance when
each acceptance entry records the claim kind, the evidence surface, the evidence
level, and a pass record for every gate in G assigned to that claim kind, and when
a route-boundary entry also records human promotion.
The deterministic dispatch function G from claim kinds to gates is part of
the operational interface. Its domain is
{formal-source, traceability, route, submission, artifact, discovery, work-selection}.
It sends formal-source claims to the build and axiom-purity gates; traceability
claims to inventory and marker-existence gates; route claims to promotion, overlap, and submission-state gates; submission claims to submission-pack gates; artifact claims to status and limitation-ledger gates; discovery claims to the pinned
discovery gate above and, for extension claims, to the theorem-content delta gate;
and work-selection claims to the scheduler gate, whose evidence surface is a
critical-path score or backlog window together with the claim kind that the selected item is meant to advance. For mixed claims, G returns the union of the
gate sets for the claim kinds present.
The route gates have separate operational meanings. The promotion gate
uses the active-paper evidence surface, namely a human promotion act producing a 20 26 _* directory with ma in .t ex and PI PE LI NE .m d ; passing means
the candidate may be daemon-visible publication work, and failing means it remains intake or source material. The overlap gate uses route identity, semanticoverlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths, and program-board state; passing means
advancement is route-disjoint or has a recorded resolution, and failing means
advancement is blocked until closure, merge, supersession, or waiting for prior
feedback. The submission-state gate uses submission markers, venue state, board
state, and submission-pack metadata; passing means the route’s publication state
permits the proposed action, and failing means the action would duplicate, race,
or under-specify an active or submitted route. The theorem-content delta gate
uses theorem inventories, hard-lint output, and delta records such as AR TI FA CT
_I NV EN TO RY .m d ; passing means that an extension claim identifies load-bearing
mathematical content, and failing means that compilation, file churn, or surface
edits may not be reported as substantive theorem growth.
A reviewable artifact for this paper is either a public repository file, a supplemental file included with the submission, or an explicit historical-evidence
boundary stating that the local path is used only as path-verified evidence and
not as a fresh command-run claim. A case witness record is path-complete when
its evidence surface gives a concrete source or artifact path for each load-bearing
component of the row. A route-overlap witness has the fields route identity,
submitted sibling, overlap reason, gate decision, and allowed next actions. An
artifact-limitation witness has the fields artifact status path, limitation ledger
path, observed limitation, gate decision, and allowed manuscript claim. A promotion witness has the fields candidate surface, human promotion evidence,
active-paper markers, gate decision, and allowed manuscript claim. A theoremcontent witness has the fields changed surface, theorem inventory or hard-lint

