and historical boundaries. Checks (iii)–(vi) prevent record-level escalations of
the declared level: missing gate-passage records, unrecorded route promotion,
unsupported discovery routing, and command-run assertion without commandrun fields. Thus an accepted record is locally checkable at the level l it declares,
and no stronger level is silently inferred by the record checker.
If a check fails, the verifier stops at a finite test. The bad subrecord is the
finite collection of fields read by that failed test: for example, K and G for a gatedomain failure, E for an evidence-surface failure, K, G , R, and the cited evidence
item for a gate-record failure, H for a promotion failure, K and D for a discoveryfield failure, or l and B for an evidence-level failure. The supplied negative fixture
is exactly the last case: it declares l = command but provides no boundary.com
mand_run_fields containing command, source commit, environment, exit code,
and log path, so the bad subrecord is the pair consisting of the evidence-level
field and the boundary field. This subrecord is independent of any surrounding
paragraph that may have described the claim.
Theorem 2 (Schema-relative finite-record acceptance exactness). Fix
the seven-kind dispatch table G , the two evidence levels, and the typed certificate
tuple C(c) = (K, l, E, H, R, D, B) of Definition 4. For every load-bearing claim
c and every finite candidate certificate C(c), the finite verifier V of Definition 2
accepts C(c) if and only if the six-clause table predicate AccG (c, C) holds. This is
a schema-relative and finite-record-relative assertion: both sides read exactly the
fields named in the fixed schema and in the finite submitted record surface, and
neither side ranges over unrecorded semantic facts about the referenced mathematics, artifacts, or gate implementations. Moreover, if V accepts a record for
c, the same record cannot certify any stronger reading c+ that adds a new claim
kind, a stronger evidence level, an omitted route crossing, an omitted discovery
interface, an omitted pass record, or a command-run assertion: such a reading
has a finite bad subrecord of the corresponding clause. Thus V is exact for this
paper’s finite record-acceptance relation over the submitted source, tables, manifests, logs, and explicitly declared historical boundaries, while remaining silent
about external semantic validity of the referenced theory or artifacts and about

An Auditable Theory Compiler

19

the implementation-level soundness of any deployed gate implementation beyond
the recorded pass rows.
Proof. The definition of V and the six clauses above use the same finite inputs.
Predicate P1 is clause A1, P2 is clause A2, and P3 is clause A3, including both
assigned-gate coverage and the requirement that each evidence pointer be tied
to the declared review surface. Predicates P4 , P5 , and P6 are exactly clauses A4,
A5, and A6. Therefore, if AccG (c, C) holds, every verifier predicate is true and
V accepts.
Conversely, suppose V accepts. Reading the successful checks in order gives
precisely clauses 1–6: the kind set and evidence level are in the permitted domains; the evidence surface is reviewer-available at the declared level; every
gate assigned by G has a pass record with the required five fields and an allowed
pointer; promotion and non-promotion are separated by H; discovery-kind claims
and only discovery-kind claims carry D; and B fixes the command-run boundary.
Hence AccG (c, C) holds.
For the final assertion, keep the accepted tuple fixed and consider a proposed
stronger reading c+ . If c+ adds a claim kind, the finite set of gates assigned by
G grows unless the added kind is vacuous in this interface; without the corresponding pass records clause 3 fails, and if the kind lies outside the seven-kind
domain clause 1 fails. If c+ raises the evidence level to command-run, clause 6
fails unless the command, commit, environment, exit code, and log path are
present. If it adds a route crossing, clause 4 fails unless H contains the humanpromotion record. If it adds discovery content, clause 5 fails unless the pinned
discovery rows are named. If it asserts a new gate-dependent conclusion without
a pass record, clause 3 fails. In each case the failed clause reads only finitely
many fields, so deleting irrelevant unread fields gives an inclusion-minimal bad
subrecord. If none of the clauses changes, then c+ is not stronger in the record
semantics: the certificate still licenses only the same kind set, same evidence
level, same gate records, same route boundary, same discovery interface, and
same command boundary.
Theorem 3 (Finite-coordinate determination and trust-boundary classifier). Fix the dispatch table G and the verifier V . Let FG be the finite schema
projection read by V from a certificate record, namely the claim-kind set, declared
evidence level, evidence surface, promotion field, gate-pass rows, discovery-source
rows, command-boundary tuple, and the assigned gate set determined by G . If
two ambient pipeline or artifact states induce the same values of all coordinates
in FG for a claim c, then they induce the same record-acceptance decision and the
same finite publication certificate for c. Therefore the exactness of Theorem 2 is
exactness only relative to this finite schema projection. No property depending on
a coordinate outside FG —for example the semantic truth of a referenced theorem,
truthful production or implementation soundness of a gate row, a fresh Lean build
result, a daemon trace, an artifact semantic fact, a theorem-environment completeness claim, or an external venue decision—is a consequence of the accepted
certificate unless that property is first encoded as an additional finite certificate

20

H. Ma and W. Zhang

coordinate and the enlarged record is accepted. In particular, record-gate soundness is a load-bearing hypothesis for Theorem 9; it is not supplied by schema
exactness alone.
Proof. The verifier V is defined as an ordered list of six checks, and each check
reads only one of the coordinates listed in FG together with the fixed finite dispatch table G . If two ambient states induce the same values on FG , then the input
tuple (K, l, E, H, R, D, B), the assigned gate set, and every submitted pointer
queried by V are identical in the two states. Each of the six checks therefore has
the same truth value in the two states, so V either accepts both records or rejects both records at the same first failed finite field. By Theorem 2, an accepted
decision is equivalent to the same six-clause record-acceptance semantics, hence
the finite certificate determined by those clauses is also the same. This proves
the schema-relative and finite-record-relative factorisation.
Now let Q be a property whose truth can vary while all coordinates in FG are
held fixed. Then there exist two ambient states with the same certificate tuple
and the same verifier decision but different truth values for Q. A conclusion whose
truth changes under such an FG -preserving substitution is not determined by the
accepted certificate. To make Q load-bearing, the operational interface must add
a finite coordinate recording the required evidence: for a build or daemon trace
this is a command-run tuple; for an artifact semantic fact this is an artifact pass
record or fresh evaluator rerun log; for theorem-environment completeness this is
an extractor command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path; and
for a venue decision this is an external evidence surface and the corresponding
route/submission record. Without that enlargement, Theorem 2 supplies only
the original finite-record conclusion.
Finally, the same argument isolates record-gate soundness. The predicate
RGSS (P, L, G ) says that recorded ledger entries actually contain the assigned
passing rows and submitted evidence pointers. A package can agree on the
schema shape of a candidate certificate while differing on whether such a recordgate-sound ledger has been supplied. Thus schema exactness checks the finite
acceptance relation after the row data are present; it does not by itself make
those rows available, truthfully produced, or semantically adequate. Theorem 9
therefore states record-gate soundness as an explicit hypothesis, and the trust
boundary is exactly the finite projection FG plus that hypothesis.
