Proposition 9 (Gate-interface separation). The promotion, overlap, submissionstate, and theorem-content delta gates are separate deterministic interfaces. None
can be replaced by passage of another one when a load-bearing claim has the corresponding component.
Proof. Each named gate has a distinct invariant and evidence surface in the operational definition. Promotion asks whether a candidate has crossed the human
publication boundary; its evidence is the active-paper directory and pipeline
metadata. Overlap asks whether the route duplicates or conflicts with a sibling
route; its evidence is route identity, overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths,
and board state. Submission-state asks whether the proposed publication action is compatible with submission markers, venue state, and submission-pack
metadata. The theorem-content delta gate asks whether an asserted extension

30

H. Ma and W. Zhang

contains load-bearing theorem growth; its evidence is an inventory, hard-lint surface, or delta record. These predicates are logically independent at the interface
level: an active paper can still overlap a submitted sibling, a route with no overlap can still have an impermissible submission state, and a compiling edit in a
permissible route can still lack theorem-content delta. Therefore a load-bearing
claim with any one of these components must pass that component’s own gate,
and passage through the others cannot supply the missing evidence.
Proposition 10 (Scheduler selection is advisory before discovery gating). Let s be a scheduler or critical-path score attached to a candidate claim c.
If s recommends selecting c, then the recommendation is a work-selection claim
and by itself can only pass the scheduler gate. If c is also asserted to be a discovery or classifier-shifting contribution in a record-gate-sound pipeline, then the
accepted mixed claim must pass both the scheduler gate and the discovery gate.
Consequently, critical-path scores may prioritize review order, and the imported
classifier-shift selection rule may break ties among otherwise equal discovery potential coordinates, but neither score nor recommendation accepts a classifiershift discovery unless the discovery gate also supplies the required certificate,
ledger, and lineage witnesses.
Proof. By the definition of G , a scheduler recommendation has claim kind workselection, so its atomic gate is the scheduler gate. The scheduler gate’s evidence
surface records a score, backlog window, or analogous critical-path priority; none
of these fields is a semantic naming certificate, a ledger witness, a DNA-complete
theorem genome, a score certificate for a discovery claim, a lineage witness, or a
not-claimed boundary. Hence scheduler passage alone records only that c is an
eligible or prioritized work item.
Now suppose that the same recorded assertion also says that c is a discovery or classifier-shifting contribution. Then the assertion is mixed: it has a
work-selection component and a discovery component. Proposition 8 therefore
requires passage through every gate in the union assigned by G , namely the
scheduler gate for the work-selection component and the pinned discovery gate
for the discovery component. The source-interface record in Table 1 includes
the imported classifier-change selection theorem only in its bounded form: with
positive classifier-change weight and all other coordinates fixed, such a selection operator favors the classifier-changing candidate. The same source-interface
record also includes pipeline safety, which requires certificate, ledger, genome,
score, lineage, and boundary witnesses for accepted discovery. Combining these
two imported interfaces gives the stated conclusion: scheduling and classifiershift scoring can advise selection order, but discovery acceptance still occurs
only through the discovery gate.
