stated record-acceptance semantics, not merely with an informal unpacking of
the tuple.
Axis and evidence-level invariant. The record just constructed exposes exactly the axes and levels on which c is allowed to rely. If a proposed consequence
of c needs evidence on a different architecture axis, Proposition 2 forces a separate submitted coordinate for that axis and a pass record for the gates assigned
to that coordinate. If the proposed consequence instead raises the evidence level
from path-verified to command-run strength, clause A6 of Definition 4 forces
the command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path into B. In
both cases, Theorem 8 identifies the added finite coordinates as the positive
extension budget for the stronger reading. If those coordinates are absent, Definition 3 identifies the proposed assertion as a properly stronger reading without
the extension coordinates required for acceptance. Theorem 2 then returns the
same original certificate and no cross-axis, cross-level, implementation, artifactsemantic, venue, or theorem-completeness consequence is certified. The failed
coordinate is a finite bad subrecord because it is either a missing kind in K, a
missing gate record in R, a missing command tuple in B, or an evidence item
outside the submitted surface E.
Gate coverage. Propositions 8 and 9 identify the required finite gate set:
formal-source claims use build and axiom gates, traceability claims use inventory and marker gates, route claims use promotion, overlap, and submission-state
gates, artifact claims use status and limitation-ledger gates, submission claims
use submission-pack gates, extension-style discovery claims use the theoremcontent delta gate together with the pinned discovery gate, work-selection claims
use the scheduler gate, and mixed claims use the union. Thus c has a prior pass
record for the gates required by its kind. The advisory-output assumption prevents an agent draft, review packet, score, or repair proposal from being promoted by prose alone: without the relevant pass records it remains demoted. The
promotion assumption similarly prevents an inactive intake seed from becoming
publication work merely because a local source path exists.
Discovery boundary. If c is a discovery claim, the discovery-routing assumption sends it through the pinned discovery-gate interface. Proposition 3 supplies the corresponding reviewer-facing snapshot interface, so this routing does
not depend on a reviewer’s access to the author’s absolute source path. The
record-file consistency certificate in Proposition 4 further shows, at the finite
record-comparison level, that the manuscript table, JSON mirror, review-bundle
manifest, digest, line count, and bundled source snapshot name the same finite
imported interface. The source-interface table records the imported statements
used here: statement-only, code-only, mechanical-expansion, and compressiononly records do not become accepted discovery claims without the required
certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses, and classifier-shift selection remains
bounded by the discovery gate as shown in Proposition 10. Finally, the constructed tuple is accepted by Proposition 6; equivalently, Theorem 2 identifies

