it with AccG . The stronger non-escalation and maximality statement is Proposition 11; the finite extension-budget normal form in Theorem 8 identifies exactly
which additional finite coordinates a stronger reading would have to add. The
current theorem is therefore the finite certificate-construction component of the
safety interface, with a separately proved budget boundary for extensions. No
current-package conclusion is used in this proof; the package instantiation appears only later, after its support-surface hypotheses have been stated.
Proposition 11 (Finite-record maximality). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 9, the constructed certificate is maximal with respect to the finite submitted
record surface. More precisely, let c be an accepted load-bearing claim and let
CertG (c) = (K, l, E, H, R, D, B) be the certificate constructed in that theorem. If
a stronger reading c+ asserts any external end-to-end compiler correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, proof-assistant correctness, current formalsource currency, dynamic artifact semantics, or venue acceptance, then either
c+ is not a distinct finite-record consequence of CertG (c), or its certificate must
strictly enlarge one of K, l, E, H, R, D, B by adding the corresponding claim kind,
evidence surface, gate pass record, promotion or venue field, discovery-interface
row, or command-run tuple. In the latter case, if the enlargement is absent, a
finite bad subrecord is returned by Theorem 2 or by one of the obstruction types
of Theorem 7.
Proof. The schema verifier used in Theorem 9 reads only the tuple (K, l, E, H, R, D, B),
the dispatch table G , and the cited submitted finite surfaces. This is exactly the
finite-coordinate determination boundary of Theorem 3. Hence two surrounding
implementations, formal-source trees, artifact runs, or venue states that agree on
those coordinates obtain the same verifier decision. A purported stronger reading which changes none of the coordinates is therefore invisible to the certificate
and cannot become a new accepted finite-record conclusion.
If the stronger reading is made load-bearing, it changes a coordinate. External
compiler, proof-assistant, formal-source-currency, or artifact-semantics claims
add claim kinds and assigned gate records; fresh executions raise the evidence
boundary to command-run data; route or venue assertions add promotion or
venue surfaces; discovery-strengthening adds source-interface rows. These are
exactly the finite fields tested by the clauses of Definition 4. When the required
field is missing, Theorem 2 supplies the failed acceptance clause, and Theorem 7
classifies the failure as a missing evidence surface, gate record, promotion field,
discovery-interface row, or command-run escalation.
