Theorem 11 is the row-frozen exactness specialization.
Corollary 2 (Finite-record boundary for external gate soundness). Under the hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem 9, the publicationsafety conclusion is invariant under replacement of the surrounding implementation by any other implementation with the same submitted acceptance entries,
evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery-interface rows, and evidence-level
boundaries. Therefore the theorem cannot be used, without an additional submitted record-gate-soundness certificate, to infer external workflow correctness,
current execution of unrecorded gates, Lean correctness, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance. Conversely, any manuscript assertion that tries to
promote the finite certificate constructor to one of those stronger implementation claims has a finite overclaim witness: either the required field is absent from
C(c), or the row’s declared evidence level exceeds the cited certificate surface.
Proof. The constructor in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem 9, reads
only the finite tuple C(c) = (K, l, E, H, R, D, B). Here K is the claim-kind set
assigned by the dispatch table, l is the declared evidence level, E is the submitted
evidence surface, H is the human-promotion field, R is the finite list of recorded
pass rows, D is the pinned discovery-interface field when the claim has a discovery
component, and B is the evidence boundary. If another implementation supplies
the same values for these fields, the same finite schema verifier receives the same
input record and therefore gives the same certificate-construction conclusion by
Proposition 6 and the finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem 2. No step of
the proof inspected an unsubmitted daemon trace, reran a proof assistant, reran
a finite-witness artifact, or queried a venue form after the recorded venue check;
those facts are not coordinates of C(c).

