Now suppose the manuscript asserted one of the stronger implementation
claims without adding a new accepted record. External workflow correctness
and current execution of unrecorded gates require pass data not present in R.
Lean correctness requires a formal-source certificate rather than the bounded
contextual rows allowed by Proposition 18. Artifact semantic validity requires a
stronger artifact row than the bounded manifest records of Theorem 15. Venue
acceptance requires a venue decision rather than the submission-pack readiness
row of Proposition 16. In each case, either the needed finite field is missing or
the row is read at a stronger evidence level than its certificate permits. The
obstruction is therefore a finite subrecord of the kind classified by Theorem 7
and Theorem 14.
Theorem 10 (Exact extension criterion for implementation-soundness
readings). Fix the dispatch table G and the finite certificate schema of Proposition 6. A reading of a load-bearing package claim as an implementation-soundness
claim is accepted by the same publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite record surface is enlarged by an implementation-soundness extension
record
Impl(c) = (K impl , E impl , Rimpl , B impl )
with the following properties.
1. K impl contains the additional implementation claim kind or claim-kind component being asserted, rather than merely reusing the original finite-record
claim kind of c.
2. E impl is a submitted finite evidence surface naming the implementation object being certified: for example the relevant source path, version or commit,
configuration, executable verifier, and implementation-log surface.
3. Rimpl contains a pass record, with evidence pointer inside E impl , for every
gate assigned by G to the enlarged claim kind K ? K impl .
4. B impl declares the evidence level of the implementation claim and contains
the command-run tuple whenever the implementation claim is asserted at
command-run strength.
When such an extension record is present, the finite certificate constructor of
Theorem 9 accepts the enlarged claim exactly at the evidence level declared by
B impl . When it is absent, every implementation-soundness reading of the same
row has a finite obstruction of one of the five kinds in Theorem 7. Thus the
central theorem is sharp: it is not an external implementation-soundness theorem
for the current package, but it gives a complete finite-record criterion for what
would have to be submitted to make such a stronger reading part of a future
package.
Proof. First suppose the extension record is present. Replace the original row for
c by the enlarged row whose claim-kind coordinate is K ? K impl , whose evidence
surface is E ? E impl , whose pass-record field is R ? Rimpl , and whose boundary
field is the original boundary together with B impl . The first condition makes
the stronger reading visible to the dispatch table rather than hiding it in prose.
The second condition supplies a finite submitted surface for the implementation

An Auditable Theory Compiler

37

object. The third condition supplies every gate record required by G for the
enlarged claim-kind set, and the fourth condition prevents a path-verified implementation record from being read as a command-run implementation certificate
without the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields.
Therefore the tuple has exactly the fields checked by Proposition 6. Applying
Theorem 9 to the enlarged row constructs an accepted finite certificate for the
implementation-soundness reading, and the accepted strength is precisely the
declared evidence level because the schema reads no unsubmitted evidence-level
coordinate.
Conversely, suppose a manuscript row is read as an implementation-soundness
claim but no extension record with the four displayed properties is submitted.
If the additional implementation claim kind is not present in the claim-kind coordinate, then the stronger reading has no assigned implementation gate and is
an unsupported escalation of the original row. If the kind is present but E impl
is absent, the claim lacks a submitted evidence surface for the implementation
object, giving obstruction type 1 of Theorem 7. If the evidence surface is present
but some gate assigned by G has no corresponding pass record in Rimpl , the
bad subrecord is type 2. If the implementation reading crosses an intake, source,
route, or venue boundary with no required promotion or decision field, the bad
subrecord is type 3. If the implementation reading is also a discovery or classifiershift reading without the pinned discovery-interface rows, the bad subrecord is
type 4. Finally, if the row asserts command-run implementation strength without
the command-run tuple inside B impl , the bad subrecord is type 5. These cases exhaust the finite schema fields by the obstruction-skeleton theorem, so absence of
the extension record is exactly absence of finite support for the stronger reading.

