Proposition 13 (Path-complete case witnesses). Suppose a case-study row
is used as load-bearing evidence for the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem 9. If the row is path-complete in the sense above, then the row supplies
exactly the evidence surface required for a path-verified historical snapshot case
claim and no evidence surface for a fresh daemon, full-source, theorem-content,
artifact-suite, benchmark, or semantic-substrate rerun. In fact every accepted
path-complete case row factors through one of four finite witness skeletons—
promotion, route/submission blocking, theorem-content non-growth, or artifactlimitation disclosure—and its admissible manuscript lesson is the unique bounded
lesson attached to that skeleton. Replacing that lesson by a fresh experiment, dynamic validation, live benchmark, or semantic artifact claim changes the skeleton
by adding a command-run or artifact-semantic coordinate, so the original row
no longer certifies the stronger claim. In particular, a promotion row is sufficient
only for the bounded claim that an intake packet is not daemon-visible publication work before human promotion; a route-overlap row is sufficient only for the
bounded claim that the overlap and submission-state gates blocked advancement
until one of the recorded next actions occurs; a theorem-content row is sufficient
only for the bounded claim that file churn or compilation did not show substantive
theorem growth; and an artifact-limitation row is sufficient only for the bounded
claim that the artifact limitation must be disclosed or must block promotion.
Proof. Let r be a path-complete case row. By definition, every load-bearing component of r has a concrete source or artifact snapshot path, directory manifest, or
explicit historical boundary. The promotion interface names the candidate surface, human-promotion evidence, active-paper markers, gate decision, and the
bounded manuscript claim; these fields are precisely the data needed to distinguish inactive intake from publication work. The route-overlap interface names
the active route, the submitted sibling that creates the chronology constraint,
the reason for overlap, the gate decision, and the finite set of permitted next
actions. These are precisely the data needed to check whether advancement is
allowed without appealing to generated prose, and the submission-state evidence
prevents a route from racing an already submitted sibling. The theorem-content
interface names the changed surface, the theorem inventory or hard-lint surface,
the substantive-content delta, the gate decision, and the allowed manuscript
claim; these fields are precisely the data needed to reject extension claims based
only on file churn or successful compilation. The artifact-limitation interface
names the status file, the limitation ledger, the observed limitation, the gate

An Auditable Theory Compiler

47

decision, and the only manuscript claim that may be carried forward. These are
precisely the data needed to prevent a failed or bounded artifact check from
being promoted into a stronger dynamic-validation claim. Lemma 2 then applies
to each such row: the named paths give the source surface, the interface fields
give the failed invariant and decision, and the last field gives the safe lesson. The
same lemma also rules out upgrading the row to a command-run claim unless
the extra command-run fields are recorded. In particular, the proof does not
rerun the publication daemon, overlap scanner, theorem-content detector, artifact suite, or GroundCompiler evaluator; it proves only that the historical path
surfaces are sufficient for the bounded lessons displayed in the table.
It remains to justify the skeleton and uniqueness assertions. The operational
interface dispatches each case row by its gate family. A row whose required gate
is promotion has exactly the promotion skeleton; a row whose required gates
are overlap and submission-state has the route/submission-blocking skeleton; a
row whose required gate is theorem-content has the non-growth skeleton; and
a row whose required gates are status and limitation-ledger has the artifactlimitation skeleton. These four skeletons are disjoint because their gate sets differ,
and the row’s safe-lesson field is part of the finite record checked by Lemma 2.
Hence, once the skeleton is fixed, the only accepted lesson is the bounded lesson
printed in that row. A fresh experiment or dynamic validation would require the
command-run boundary fields in Definition 2; an artifact-semantic claim would
require an additional artifact pass record or semantic evaluator surface. Those
coordinates are not read by the path-complete row and are not present in the
four skeletons. The stronger reading is therefore rejected by Theorem 3 together
with the finite obstruction skeleton, while the original bounded historical lesson
remains accepted.

