missing formal-source gates or missing command-run fields.
Proof. By the dispatch rule in the operational interface, an accepted formalsource claim would have claim kind formal-source and therefore would require
pass records for both the build gate and the axiom-purity gate. The current
acceptance ledger has exactly the six rows a1 , . . . , a6 displayed in Table 2; none
has claim kind formal-source. The machine-readable schema mirrors this fact:
its claim_kind_domain includes formal-source, and its gate_dispatch.form
al-source field names build and axiom-purity, but the current pass-record
ledger uses a1 for traceability, a5 for rejected stronger discovery/theorem-content
claims, and contains no accepted formal-source row with build and axiom-purity
pass records.
It follows that the only positive certificate available for rows pc3 and pc4
is contextual. Row a1 supplies inventory and marker-existence records for the
pinned source-interface snapshot, so the paper may say which Lean-related
source-interface vocabulary is being imported. Row a5 supplies the boundary
that file churn, compilation, or a fake extension is not theorem growth or discovery, so the paper may explain why formal-looking material is not promoted without its gate records. The certificate schema and primary-claim inventory make
the boundary checkable by naming the absent stronger level: a formal-source
acceptance would need build and axiom-purity pass records, and a commandrun version would additionally need command, source commit, environment, exit
code, and log path. Since those fields are absent from the current package, Proposition 6 rejects the stronger reading by a finite missing-gate or evidence-level
subrecord. The proposition therefore certifies exactly the bounded contextual
Lean interface and no fresh Lean build or full-source axiom audit.
For the converse boundary, suppose a revised package did add a formal-source
certificate row for one of these sentences. By the deterministic dispatch rule, the

