Theorem 13 (Formal-source promotion obstruction). In the current presentation package, no manuscript sentence about the Lean/BEDC source interface can be promoted from bounded contextual interface evidence to an accepted
formal-source claim unless the certificate is enlarged by an accepted formal-source
row containing build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records. If a fresh execution reading is also asserted, the certificate must additionally contain the command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code,
and log path required for command-run evidence. With the present six-row package ledger, every such promotion attempt has a finite bad subrecord and is rejected
by the schema verifier.
Proof. The dispatch table sends a formal-source claim to the two gates named in
Proposition 18: build and axiom-purity. Therefore any accepted formal-source
version of rows pc3 or pc4 would need a certificate whose kind set contains
formal-source and whose pass-record field R contains one pass record for each
of these two gates. By Theorem 11, the current package has exactly the accepted certificates a1 , . . . , a6 . None has claim kind formal-source; none contains
build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records; and the only positive Lean-facing
surface is the bounded traceability and source-interface context identified in
Proposition 18.
If the promoted reading also asserts a fresh Lean build, current full-source
marker audit, full-source axiom audit, or command-run theorem-source check,
then Definition 2 requires the command-run tuple: command, source commit or
final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log path. The boundary fields in Tables 3 and 11 state that no such command-run tuple is present
for the Lean/BEDC source theorem semantics. Thus a formal-source promotion
fails check (iii) of the finite verifier by missing required gate pass records; a freshexecution promotion fails check (vi) by unsupported command-run escalation. In
either case Theorem 7 extracts the failed fields as a finite bad subrecord. Hence
the current package keeps the Lean/BEDC material at the declared bounded
contextual level unless new formal-source and command-run evidence rows are
actually added.
Theorem 14 (Evidence-level overclaim obstruction). Fix the certificate
schema, dispatch function, and evidence-level vocabulary of this paper. Let c be
a load-bearing claim whose accepted certificate has level path-verified and whose
boundary field B records that no command-run assertion is made. Then every

