it is not merely the finite certificate-construction claim accepted by the finiterecord interface theorem, Theorem 9. It S
needs pass records showing that the
relevant implementation ran every gate in k?K G (k). Those records are not supplied by the path-verified certificate for c, so the minimal bad subrecord is a missing pass-record obstruction of type 2. If c+ asserts Lean correctness, full-source
rebuild success, or axiom-purity, then it is a formal-source or command-run
claim. Proposition 18 and Theorem 13 identify the required build, axiom-purity,
and command-run fields; when they are absent, the minimal bad subrecord is
type 2 for missing formal-source gates or type 5 for command-run escalation.
If c+ asserts end-to-end semantic validity of artifacts beyond the named
manifest and evaluator surfaces, then it has an artifact component not covered
