Theorem 21 (Manual theorem-inventory boundary). The file th eo re
m_ in ve nt or y. js on is a manual Stage-A inventory record rather than a deterministic theorem-environment extractor output. Its accepted role in the current

An Auditable Theory Compiler

77

theorem chain is finite obligation traceability: it enumerates the theorem-like obligations selected by the Stage-A review, including the reported count of sixty-one
displayed theorem-like entries, and connects that finite list to the manifest and
acceptance ledger. The inventory indexes obligations; the evidence for those obligations is supplied by the corresponding TeX statements and proofs in this source,
together with external records only where a statement explicitly cites them. It is
not an executable completeness certificate for all LATEX theorem environments
in ma in .t ex ; no extractor command, extractor log, or theorem-environment
coverage claim is part of the current submitted support surface. Consequently
any claim that the inventory is complete because a deterministic extractor found
all theorem-like environments, or that the ledger itself semantically checks the
printed proofs, is rejected by the current finite-record interface, while the weaker
claim that it is a manual non-semantic ledger is accepted. The current package
may therefore cite the ledger only as an indexed support coordinate in the theorem
chain of Theorem 22, never as automated completeness, extractor soundness, or
proof-checking evidence.
Proof. The support records named in Table 10 are finite manifest and ledger
records. The root manifest exposes the path theorem_inventory.json; acceptanceledger entry a7 records the inventory, marker-existence, and submission-pack
gates for the bounded theorem-ledger claim; and the certificate verifier checks
only record shape, gate coverage, evidence pointers, and file availability. None of
these records names an extractor program, a command line that scans main.tex
for theorem-like environments, or a log comparing extractor output with the
inventory bytes. By the command-result rule in the scope contract, such an
executable completeness claim would require its command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path. Those coordinates are absent.
The remaining accepted reading is exactly the manual one. A finite manual inventory can still be useful: it fixes the Stage-A obligation surface that
the submission chooses to expose and lets the certificate ledger prevent hidden
prose-only references to that surface. But because no deterministic extractor is
recorded, the inventory cannot discharge the stronger claim that every theorem,
proposition, lemma, corollary, or definition environment in the TeX source was
independently discovered by an executable scan. The printed mathematical obligations remain proved by the corresponding statements and proofs in this source,
with external records serving as evidence only for the finite-record, commandboundary, manifest, and support-surface claims that cite them. The inventory
therefore performs only the indexing step: it lists the obligations and connects
that list to the acceptance ledger, while proof and evidence remain on separate
coordinates. Thus theorem_inventory.json supplies only the bounded manual traceability coordinate stated in the theorem. The stronger deterministic
extraction reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord, namely the absent
extractor command and log tuple. The weaker manual-ledger reading has no such
bad subrecord because its only asserted coordinates are the finite list, the manifest path, and the acceptance-ledger pointer. Hence the finite interface accepts
exactly the non-semantic manual boundary and rejects extractor completeness.

