Proposition 22 (Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure). Suppose a Stage-A pass regenerates either th eo re m_ in ve nt or y. js on or th eo
re m_ in ve nt or y. md after the package has selected the exact-inclusion support branch. Then the final support-surface claims of Theorem 17, the submitted
theorem-ledger claim of Theorem 22, and the command boundary of Proposition 26 remain closed only after the package refreshes the corresponding SHA256 rows in re vi ew _b un dl e/ FI NA L_ DI GE ST S_ SH A2 56 .m d and reruns the
finite record verifiers whose records name that digest manifest or inventory coordinate. Once those rows and verifier logs are refreshed, the regeneration creates
no new mathematical or executable extractor claim: it only changes the bytecoordinate of the manual theorem-obligation ledger inside the already selected
finite support surface.
Proof. The selected support branch is byte-level exact inclusion. In that branch,
Theorem 17 identifies the submitted support surface by relative paths together
with the digest rows in review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md. The
theorem inventory is one of those paths: Table 10 exposes theorem_inventory.
json through the root manifest and acceptance-ledger entry a7, while the final
digest record also lists the human-readable companion theorem_inventory.md.
Therefore regenerating either file after the digest record was written changes a
finite coordinate used by the exact support-surface relation. Until the SHA-256
rows are recomputed, the digest record denotes the old bytes, and the regenerated
inventory is not the same finite certificate file as the one named by the final
packaging record.
The required reruns are exactly the finite record verifiers at the package
boundary. The certificate-record verifier checks that the manifest-required paths,
including the theorem-inventory coordinate, are present and covered by the accepted gate records; the source-interface and primary-claim verifiers record their
command boundary against the same final digest manifest. Rerunning those
commands after the digest refresh supplies fresh source state, environment, exit
code, and log-path coordinates for the command-boundary table. No verifier
in this list scans main.tex for theorem-like environments or proves theorem semantics. Hence the refreshed state is closed precisely as a finite record-and-digest
package, and by Proposition 21 it remains a manual Stage-A obligation ledger
rather than an executable theorem-environment extraction certificate.
