Proof. The support surface is finite and can be inspected coordinate by coordinate. The root manifest now contains the key theorem_inventory with value
theorem_inventory.json. The certificate schema includes the same key in
its required manifest-file map. Therefore a certificate-record verification of the
enlarged package must check that the manifest exposes this root-level inventory
path and that the file is present. This removes the previous possibility of silently
citing a root-level Stage-A artifact outside the support surface.
The acceptance ledger contains the additional entry a7. Its accepted claim is
exactly the bounded submitted theorem-obligation ledger claim; its claim kinds
are traceability and submission. Under the schema’s gate dispatch, those
kinds require the inventory, marker-existence, and submission-pack gates. Entry
a7 supplies precisely those three pass records: the inventory gate points to th
eorem_inventory.json, the marker-existence gate points to Table 10, and the
submission-pack gate points to review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.jso
n. Thus the ledger supplies a finite route from the theorem-inventory file to the
submitted certificate records rather than a prose-only reference.
It remains to identify the boundary. The schema-level verifier checks finite
record shape, required manifest paths, gate coverage, evidence pointers, and
evidence levels. None of those checks is a semantic proof of the theorem-like
environments in this source, and none reruns Lean, the publication daemon, the
Rule110/GroundCompiler artifacts, or an independent theorem-environment extractor. Proposition 21 supplies the missing classification: the inventory is an
accepted manual Stage-A ledger and not an executable completeness certificate. The theorem-ledger claim is therefore admissible only at the path-verified
evidence level stated in a7. The mathematical content of the article remains
carried by the printed definitions, propositions, lemmas, theorems, and proofs,
and external records carry only the finite evidence claims that cite them. The
integrated inventory gives the submitted finite obligation surface against which
those theorem-like claims are enumerated; it indexes the obligations and does
not prove them. This is exactly the bounded claim stated in the theorem.
